      "problem": "The projection of the intersection between the parabolic cylinder $4(x-3)^{2}=12(z+1)$ and the plane $y=2$ onto the $xz$-plane is a parabola. What is the vertex of the parabola?",
      "worked_solution": "To find the intersection of the cylinder and the plane $y=2,$ we substitute $y=2$ into the equation of the cylinder. However, since the equation has no (explicit) $y$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-3)^{2} & =12(z+1) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =3(z+1) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot (z+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a parabola in the $xz$-plane with the vertex at $(3,-1).$",
